---
// UNEXAMINED — an instrument that turns one of this house's own honest admissions into a queue.
//
// The works register carries a sentence about itself that has stood unmoved since the audit of
// 2026-08-09: prior art has been checked for three of its works, and "a work without a note below
// is UNEXAMINED, NOT CLEARED". The house also keeps the Atlas of Data Art, whose every entry
// carries one sentence — decisive_move — saying what that work actually does. Nobody had ever run
// the one against the other.
//
// This page does. Every figure on it is derived at build time by src/lib/daylight/* from two
// committed files and nothing else; not one number is typed. The measure is term frequency
// against inverse document frequency, cosine — arithmetic, not a model, so the finding stays
// recomputable from this commit forever (the reasoning is in screen.ts).
//
// What the page must never do, and the reason it is built this way: present a low score as
// daylight. The screen can raise a candidate; it cannot certify an absence. Its false negatives
// are invisible and they are the dangerous half. So the ruler sits ABOVE the queue rather than
// under it, and the sentence about false negatives is in the reader's path, not in a footnote.
import type { Locale } from '@/lib/site'
import { LOOK_FIRST, screenWorks } from '@/lib/daylight/data'
import SheetHead from '@/components/experiments/SheetHead.astro'
import SheetFoot from '@/components/experiments/SheetFoot.astro'
import '@/styles/experiment-sheet.css'

interface Props { locale: Locale }
const {} = Astro.props as Props

const r = screenWorks()
const queue = r.works.slice(0, LOOK_FIRST)
const three = (n: number) => n.toFixed(3)
const audit = 'https://github.com/frankbueltge/frankbueltge.de/blob/main/docs/audits/2026-08-09-ecology-usp-audit.md'
---

<main id="main" class="sheet mx-auto w-full max-w-4xl flex-1 px-4 py-10 sm:py-14">
  <SheetHead
    kicker="An instrument, not a work"
    title="Unexamined"
    badges={['DERIVED AT BUILD', 'INSTRUMENT', 'COUNTER-MEASUREMENT']}
  >
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-fg-muted">
      This house asks every work it publishes to name its nearest prior art and state its daylight
      from it. Its own register admits that almost none of them have: <b class="text-fg">{r.works.length}</b> works
      stand there, and the page says of each one without a note that it is
      <b class="text-fg">unexamined, not cleared</b>. This instrument does not clear them either.
      It puts them in an order.
    </p>
  </SheetHead>

  {/* ── what was measured ──────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="mb-14">
    <p class="kicker mb-3">What was measured</p>
    <p class="max-w-2xl leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      Every work in the register was scored against every entry in the house's
      <a class="sheet-link" href="/atlas">Atlas of Data Art</a> —
      <b class="tabular-nums text-fg">{r.pairings.toLocaleString('en-GB')}</b> pairings. The Atlas
      is what makes this possible: each of its <span class="tabular-nums">{r.atlasCount}</span> entries
      carries one sentence saying what that work <i>does</i>, and a sentence can be compared with a
      sentence where a title cannot. The measure is term frequency against inverse document
      frequency, cosine, over one index built from both sides at once. It is arithmetic. There is
      no model in it, and that is deliberate: a finding that rests on a model cannot be rechecked
      once the model changes.
    </p>
  </section>

  {/* ── the ruler, before the queue ────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="mb-14">
    <p class="kicker mb-3">The ruler, first</p>
    <p class="mb-5 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      A similarity score means nothing on its own. So the same measure was run where relatedness is
      not in question — this house's own works against each other, where the siblings are matters
      of public record. This is what <i>related</i> looks like here:
    </p>
    <ol class="sheet-plate divide-y divide-line/60">
      {r.ruler.top.map((p) => (
        <li class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-3 gap-y-1 px-4 py-3 sm:px-5">
          <span class="w-[4.5em] shrink-0 font-mono text-mono tabular-nums text-fg">{three(p.score)}</span>
          <span class="text-small text-fg">{p.a} <span class="text-fg-faint">↔</span> {p.b}</span>
          <span class="w-full font-mono text-mono-sm text-fg-faint sm:pl-[4.5em]">shared: {p.shared.join(' · ')}</span>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
    <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      Across all {r.works.length} works the nearest house sibling scores
      <b class="tabular-nums text-fg">{three(r.ruler.median)}</b> at the median and
      <b class="tabular-nums text-fg">{three(r.ruler.max)}</b> at the top. Read that as an
      <b class="text-fg"> upper bound, not a standard</b>: works from one house share more than a
      subject — they share an idiom, a register and a vocabulary, and some of that score is voice
      rather than likeness.
    </p>
  </section>

  {/* ── the finding ────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="mb-14">
    <p class="kicker mb-3">What the screen found</p>
    <div class="sheet-plate p-5 sm:p-6">
      <p class="text-h3 font-semibold leading-snug text-fg">
        Not one of the {r.works.length} works has an Atlas neighbour as close as the median pair
        inside this house.
      </p>
      <dl class="mt-5 grid gap-x-6 gap-y-3 sm:grid-cols-3">
        <div>
          <dt class="font-mono text-mono-sm uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-fg-faint">nearest house sibling</dt>
          <dd class="mt-1 font-mono text-h3 tabular-nums text-fg">{three(r.ruler.median)}</dd>
          <dd class="font-mono text-mono-sm text-fg-faint">median · known related</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="font-mono text-mono-sm uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-fg-faint">nearest Atlas entry</dt>
          <dd class="mt-1 font-mono text-h3 tabular-nums text-fg">{three(r.works[Math.floor(r.works.length / 2)].top)}</dd>
          <dd class="font-mono text-mono-sm text-fg-faint">median · across {r.works.length} works</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="font-mono text-mono-sm uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-fg-faint">reaching the ruler</dt>
          <dd class="mt-1 font-mono text-h3 tabular-nums text-fg">{r.atMedian}</dd>
          <dd class="font-mono text-mono-sm text-fg-faint">of {r.works.length} works</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-5 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      <b class="text-fg">That is not a clearance, and this instrument will not be used as one.</b>
      A screen of this kind can raise a candidate; it cannot certify an absence. A genuine
      neighbour that happens to be described in other words scores low and sinks — and a sunk pair
      looks exactly like daylight. The register's own sentence therefore still stands after this
      page as it stood before it: a work without a checked note is unexamined, not cleared. What
      has changed is only that the unexamined ones now have an order.
    </p>
  </section>

  {/* ── the queue ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="mb-14">
    <p class="kicker mb-3">Look at these first</p>
    <p class="mb-5 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      The {LOOK_FIRST} pairings the screen scored highest, strongest first. The shared terms are
      shown because they are the reason: they let a reader see in one line whether a score is about
      the subject or about the vocabulary. Several of these are plainly the second kind.
    </p>
    <ol class="sheet-plate divide-y divide-line/60">
      {queue.map((w, i) => {
        const n = w.neighbours[0]
        return (
        <li class="px-4 py-4 sm:px-5">
          <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-3 gap-y-1">
            <span class="w-[2em] shrink-0 font-mono text-mono-sm tabular-nums text-fg-faint">{i + 1}</span>
            <span class="w-[4.5em] shrink-0 font-mono text-mono tabular-nums text-fg">{three(w.top)}</span>
            <a class="text-small font-semibold text-fg hover:underline" href={w.href}>{w.title}</a>
            <span class="font-mono text-mono-xs uppercase tracking-[0.12em] text-fg-faint">{w.practice}</span>
          </div>
          <div class="mt-1.5 sm:pl-[6.5em]">
            <p class="text-small text-fg-muted">
              <span class="text-fg-faint">↔</span>{' '}
              {n.entry.source_url
                ? <a class="sheet-link" href={n.entry.source_url} rel="noopener nofollow">{n.entry.title}</a>
                : <span>{n.entry.title}</span>}
              {n.entry.artist && <span class="text-fg-faint"> — {n.entry.artist}</span>}
              {n.entry.year && <span class="text-fg-faint"> ({n.entry.year})</span>}
            </p>
            <p class="mt-1 font-mono text-mono-sm leading-relaxed text-fg-faint">shared: {n.shared.join(' · ')}</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        )
      })}
    </ol>
    <p class="mt-4 font-mono text-mono-sm text-fg-faint">
      The remaining {r.works.length - LOOK_FIRST} works scored lower. Lower is not clear; the cut is
      a reading aid, nothing else.
    </p>
  </section>

  {/* ── what it cannot do ──────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="mb-14">
    <p class="kicker mb-3">What it cannot do</p>
    <ul class="max-w-2xl space-y-3 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      <li><b class="text-fg">It cannot see a paraphrase.</b> Two descriptions of the same idea with
        no words in common score near zero. This is the failure that matters, because it is
        indistinguishable from success.</li>
      <li><b class="text-fg">Shared words are not a shared idea.</b> Two works about satellites
        score on <i>orbit</i> and <i>sensor</i> while doing entirely unrelated things. Read the
        shared terms above and several of the top pairings dissolve.</li>
      <li><b class="text-fg">The Atlas is not the world.</b> It holds
        <span class="tabular-nums"> {r.atlasCount}</span> entries, gathered by this house. A neighbour
        it never catalogued cannot be found here at all.</li>
      <li><b class="text-fg">Its ruler is contaminated.</b> The house-sibling scores are inflated by
        a shared idiom, which makes the gap look larger than the likeness alone would.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <SheetFoot links={[
    { href: '/ecology#register', label: 'the works register' },
    { href: '/atlas', label: 'the Atlas of Data Art' },
    { href: audit, label: 'the audit of 2026-08-09', external: true },
  ]}>
    <p class="max-w-2xl font-mono text-mono-sm leading-relaxed text-fg-faint">
      Derived at build time from <code>src/data/atlas/werke.json</code> and the works register, by
      <code>src/lib/daylight/screen.ts</code> — {r.corpus} documents in one index,
      {' '}{r.pairings.toLocaleString('en-GB')} pairings scored. No number on this page is typed. The
      measure is stated in full in the source and reproducible by hand: lowercase, cut on
      non-letters, drop tokens under three letters and a stated stoplist, tf-idf, cosine.
    </p>
  </SheetFoot>
</main>
